<a href="https://github.com/AJBogo9/road-cycling-finland" class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm">GitHub</a>

I like road cycling and wanted quiet countryside roads with good asphalt that I can reach by train or metro from Espoo. Väylävirasto, the Finnish Transport Infrastructure Agency, publishes the data for this: speed limits, daily traffic counts, the wearing course and measured pavement condition for state roads. I built a pipeline that joins those layers, scores each stretch of road and maps the ones that pass.

<iframe src="https://ajbogo9.github.io/road-cycling-finland/?embed" title="Map of quiet roads for road cycling in Uusimaa" loading="lazy" allow="fullscreen" style="display:block; width:100%; height:480px; margin:0 0 1.25rem; border:0; border-radius:8px;"></iframe>

Darker blue means a higher score. Click a road to see its speed limit, traffic, condition and nearest station. Zoom with Ctrl and the scroll wheel or with two fingers. The corner button shows the map full screen; on an iPhone, use the Open the map button above instead.

## What it does

A stretch of road passes when it has:

- asphalt, normal or soft;
- a speed limit of 80 km/h or less;
- at most 1000 vehicles a day;
- a pavement condition of class 3 or better, on a scale of 1 to 5.

The score from 0 to 100 weighs traffic most, then condition, speed limit and surface. In the first run on 5 October 2026, 2,693 of the 9,130 km of state road in the mapped area passed.

## How it works

Väylävirasto stores each attribute as intervals of a road address: road number, road part, and distance along the part. The road geometry carries the same address at its vertices, so my Python pipeline (geopandas, shapely) cuts each road part at every interval end and gives each piece the values that cover it. Where two values overlap, such as the two sides of a road, the piece keeps the one worse for cycling.

Rail stations come from Fintraffic's Digitraffic API and metro stations from HSL's GTFS feed. A rail station counts only if a passenger train left it on the day of the fetch, because the station list also marks stations with no regular passenger trains. A GitHub Actions workflow fetches the data again every week and publishes the map to GitHub Pages.
